{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "Tales from the Workplace", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "After a Long Week", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 11, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 6, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.3", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Accurately use information about people's backgrounds in a wider variety of communicative situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of a range of grammar and vocabulary, and with the appropriate level of formality and purpose, coherence and cohesion for the context.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information and aspects in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_8"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Gerunds and Infinitives", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<p>A <span class=\"strong\">gerund</span> is a verb ending with <span class=\"italic\">-ing</span> (<span class=\"italic\">doing</span>).<br/>An <span class=\"strong\">infinitive</span> is <span class=\"italic\">to</span> + verb (<span class=\"italic\">to do</span>).</p>There are rules about which verbs you can use with gerunds and which verbs you can use with infinitives.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Verbs commonly followed by a gerund:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Verbs that express <span class=\"strong\">likes/dislikes</span>:<ul><li>like</li><li>love</li><li>enjoy</li><li>dislike</li><li>hate</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">On the weekend, <span class=\"strong\">people enjoy</span> being active.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The verbs <span class=\"italic\">like</span>/<span class=\"italic\">love</span>/<span class=\"italic\">hate</span> can also be followed by an infinitive.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Some other verbs that are followed by a gerund:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<ul><li>anticipate</li><li>consider</li><li>delay</li><li>complete</li><li>involve</li><li>suggest</li><li>risk</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use gerunds after prepositions:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">After studying</span> 17 years of weather records 24 hours a day along the East Coast of North America, a pattern was found.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "These are some verbs that are followed by an infinitive:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<ul><li>hope</li><li>proceed</li><li>seem</li><li>need</li><li>attempt</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">Does it just <span class=\"strong\">seem to rain</span> more on Saturdays?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use infinitives after adjectives. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">By the weekend, when most people are <span class=\"strong\">ready to pursue</span> a day of pleasure, the clouds burst and rain falls.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "We had better start to care more about the ecosystem.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If the people who complain about weekend rain are the same people who cause it, they should be interested in finding a way to create sunnier Saturdays.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Then, just as we attempt to have balance in our week with sun and fun, it starts to rain.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "The Passive", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use an active structure when you want the \u2018doer' of the action to be the subject.<br/>You use a passive structure when you want the \u2018receiver' of the action to be the subject. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Active sentence = <span class=\"italic\">The sun</span> (subject) <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">heats</span> the pollutants</span> (object) <span class=\"italic\">as they rise into the air.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Passive sentence = <span class=\"italic\">As pollutants </span>(object) <span class=\"italic\">rise into the air, they <span class=\"strong\">are heated</span></span> (by the sun). <br/>It is not necessary to mention the object in a passive sentence. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You make the passive form of a verb by using tenses of the verb<span class=\"italic\"> to be</span> followed by the past participle.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The Simple Present passive form is: <br/> Subject + <span class=\"italic\">is</span>/<span class=\"italic\">are</span> + past participle", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">Picnics <span class=\"strong\">are canceled</span>, and football matches <span class=\"strong\">are postponed</span>.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The Simple Past passive form is: <br/> Subject + <span class=\"italic\">was</span>/<span class=\"italic\">were</span> + past participle", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">After studying 17 years of weather records, a pattern <span class=\"strong\">was found</span>.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "After studying 17 years of weather records 24 hours a day along the East Coast of North America, a pattern was found.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "As pollutants rise into the air, they are heated by the sun.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Picnics are canceled, and football matches are postponed.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "active", "type": "name"}, {"content": "having energetic activity", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: On the weekend, people enjoy being active.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words to review.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "nature walk", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a walk on a nature trail with an experienced guide", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We organize our nature walks, take out our tennis rackets, and pack our suntan lotion. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "balance", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a state where things are of equal importance or force", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Just as we attempt to restore balance in our week with sun and fun, it starts to rain.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "24 hours a day", "type": "name"}, {"content": "all day; around the clock", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: After studying 17 years of weather records 24 hours a day along the East Coast of North America, a pattern was found.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "average ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "usual or ordinary standard, level, or quantity", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Average rainfall was lowest on Mondays and increased throughout the week until Saturday.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "to pursue", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to seek to attain or accomplish a goal, especially over a long period", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: By the weekend, most people are ready to pursue a day of pleasure.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le06ob11aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Imagine that you have a busy, stressful job.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Plan a weekend of activities to help you balance your week.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Create a schedule with all your activities.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Compare your schedule with another student. Which schedule do you like better?", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I talk about jobs, work and staying healthy.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about my attitudes toward an action.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about what I should, ought to, had better or am supposed to do.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I make a weekend schedule of activities.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "_data": [], "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"image": "engenn8le06ob11re2img01.jpg", "time": "0", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"image": "engenn8le06ob11re2img01.jpg", "time": "", "id": 1, "text": "The work week is over. On the weekend, people enjoy being active. We organize our nature walks, take out our tennis rackets, pack our suntan lotion. Then, just as we attempt to restore balance in our week with sun and fun, it starts to rain. Does it just seem to rain more on Saturdays, or is there any proof that Saturday really is the rainiest day of the week?"}, {"image": "engenn8le06ob11re2img01.jpg", "time": "", "id": 2, "text": "After studying 17 years of weather records 24 hours a day along the East Coast of North America, a pattern was found: average rainfall was lowest on Mondays and increased throughout the week until Saturday, which had more rain than any other day."}, {"image": "engenn8le06ob11re2img01.jpg", "time": "", "id": 3, "text": "What is the explanation? The scientists blame cars. Air pollution rising from cars builds up throughout the week as people commute to and from work. As pollutants rise into the air, they are heated by the sun. Clouds form. By the weekend, when most people are ready to pursue a day of pleasure, the clouds burst and rain falls. Picnics are canceled, and football matches are postponed."}, {"image": "engenn8le06ob11re2img01.jpg", "time": "", "id": 4, "text": "If the people who complain about weekend rain are the same people who cause it, they should be interested in finding a way to create sunnier Saturdays. People ought to be more willing to confront the environmental issues they create. We had better start to care more about the ecosystem. We are supposed to be creating a better world!"}], "audiosrc": "engenn8le06ob11re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn8le06ob11re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "id": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "video", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"image": "", "time": "0", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"image": "", "time": "100", "id": 1, "text": "The work week is over. On the weekend, people enjoy being active. We organize our nature walks, take out our tennis rackets, pack our suntan lotion. Then, just as we attempt to restore balance in our week with sun and fun, it starts to rain. Does it just seem to rain more on Saturdays, or is there any proof that Saturday really is the rainiest day of the week?"}, {"image": "", "time": "22500", "id": 2, "text": "After studying 17 years of weather records 24 hours a day along the East Coast of North America, a pattern was found: average rainfall was lowest on Mondays and increased throughout the week until Saturday, which had more rain than any other day."}, {"image": "", "time": "39800", "id": 3, "text": "What is the explanation? The scientists blame cars. Air pollution rising from cars builds up throughout the week as people commute to and from work. As pollutants rise into the air, they are heated by the sun. Clouds form. By the weekend, when most people are ready to pursue a day of pleasure, the clouds burst and rain falls. Picnics are canceled, and football matches are postponed."}, {"image": "", "time": "65800", "id": 4, "text": "If the people who complain about weekend rain are the same people who cause it, they should be interested in finding a way to create sunnier Saturdays. People ought to be more willing to confront the environmental issues they create. We had better start to care more about the ecosystem. We are supposed to be creating a better world!"}], "video_src": "engenn8le06ob11re2vid01"}, "type": "video"}], "id": 2}], "exercises": 